description "Boot the installed FreeDOS disk and report it ready"
platform dos
machine  stopped
timeout  2m

# The handoff idiom, and the one thing the other two examples do not
# show: this script leaves the machine RUNNING.
#
# `freedos-install` and `freedos-verify` both end with the guest
# powered off, because each has finished with it. A readiness script
# has the opposite job — it hands a live machine to whatever comes
# next, which is a harness driving `exec`, `put-file` and `get-file`
# against a guest that is already at a prompt.
#
# What "ready" means is the workflow's own business, never
# reliquary's, and this is the whole of what it means here: the
# installed system reached a DOS prompt. A workflow needing more —
# a TSR resident, a driver bound, a network stack answering — says so
# by waiting for its own evidence and setting the variable after.
start
wait "C:\>"

# The last step, deliberately: the variable is the promise, so it is
# set only once everything above it has held. The host reads it with
# `rlq get-machine-var ready`, or contracts the run on it in one call
# with `rlq run-script ready --blueprint freedos --expect ready=yes`.
#
# The key is plain `ready` rather than `rlq.ready`: the `rlq` and
# `reliquary` namespaces are reserved for reliquary's own names, and
# a script setting one is a static error. Your variables are yours.
set ready "yes"
